# converted from 08-the-hollow.md
@title The Hollow
@bg hollow
@mood grief

narrate The woman's name was Petra and she had the Fever, and Cael had his hands inside her chest when the Hollow burned.
narrate He did not know it was the Hollow at first. He knew that something had gone wrong in the south — the thinning, the wrongness, a great deal of memory being spent at once — and he stood in a doorway three miles north with a stranger's ribs under his palms and did the arithmetic he had been doing for eleven years.
narrate He went north.
narrate He told the boy that afterwards, in the reeds, and he told it as the truth, and it was the truth, and it was not the reason.
narrate The reason took him twelve more years to say out loud, and he only ever said it once, in a chapter of his life that has not been written yet.
narrate The reason was this: I wanted him to see it.
narrate He wanted the boy to stand in the reeds and watch the Order put arrows into a woman who had gone back for four people who could not walk. He wanted the boy to learn, in one night, what Cael had learned in eleven years — that the arithmetic is real, that it is not a lie the Order tells itself, that the world is genuinely built that way and there is no version of it where everyone lives.
narrate He wanted the boy to hate it.
narrate Because Cael had spent eleven years learning that the counting is real, and had come out of it able to do it, calmly, with his hands steady. And the boy, he was almost certain, would come out of it unable to do it — would come out of it refusing — and that refusal was the thing Cael had been looking for in the dark for eleven years, in himself, and had never once found.
narrate He went north because four people needed him.
narrate He stayed north because he wanted a nineteen-year-old to watch a shrine burn.
narrate Both of those were true. He had stopped expecting the truth to be singular somewhere around the fourth year.
narrate He came back at dawn and found the boy in the reeds with nine survivors and a dog.
narrate The boy had got nine people out. Cael counted them from the treeline before he walked in — nine, and the dog, and no Maeve — and stood in the trees for a moment with his hand on a trunk.
narrate Nine. He had saved four. The boy had saved nine and had not done any arithmetic at all; he had simply gone back, and gone back, and gone back, until there was nobody left to go back for.
narrate Cael walked out of the trees.
narrate The boy hit him.
narrate He took it. He had known it was coming from about forty feet away and he had decided, on the walk in, to take it, because he had earned it and because taking it was the only honest thing available to him.
narrate He went down on one knee in the wet ground.
corin neutral Good,
corin neutral That's the correct first move. I did the same thing, my first time.
narrate The boy sat with Maeve for a long time.
narrate Cael did not follow him at first. He stood at the edge of the reeds and let him have it, because even he could see that a man needs to sit with his dead without an audience.
narrate And then he went and sat down six feet away — not beside him, six feet, the distance of a man who has learned exactly how much room to give — and said nothing.
narrate They sat there until the sun was up.
narrate And Cael looked at the row of bodies in the reeds — four of them, Maeve and three patients — and thought about a shrine he had not been in for eleven years, and a woman who had sat on the floor beside a chair and told him that he would not survive this and would get up anyway.
cael neutral I knew her,
narrate The boy did not answer.
cael neutral She came every day for a month, after Ilsa. She never once told me it would be all right.
cael neutral She said: you will not survive this, and you will get up anyway, and those are not contradictory.
narrate The reeds moved.
cael neutral I have thought about that sentence every day for twelve years,
cael neutral I still don't know if she was right.
corin neutral She was right,
narrate And Cael — who had spent eleven years being the most certain man in the Mire, who had stood at a hole in a field and done the arithmetic and never once flinched — found that he could not answer that.
narrate He stood up and offered the boy his hand.
narrate The boy looked at it for a long moment.
narrate And then he took it.
narrate Cael had planned for this to be the moment.
narrate He had planned, over eleven weeks, for the boy to take his hand in the reeds outside a burned village, and for that to be the beginning of the end — the moment the boy stopped being a soldier and started being the thing that Cael had spent eleven years arranging.
narrate It was not the moment.
narrate He understood that almost immediately, standing there with a nineteen-year-old's hand in his, and it took him four more days to work out why.
narrate The boy had not taken his hand because he had decided to become anything. He had taken it because he was nineteen, and he was tired, and everyone he had ever loved was dead, and there was a man standing in front of him offering a hand, and he took it the way you take a hand.
narrate That was the difference between them. Cael had spent his whole life making decisions. Corin simply did things, and then had to live with them, and the living with them was what made him.
narrate Cael stood in the reeds with the boy's hand in his and thought:
narrate And he felt, for the second time in eleven weeks, the thing he had felt in the trees at Sallow's Rest.
narrate Hope. And this time he let himself name it.
